WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:05.399
Speaker 1: So Joe Biden is ending negotiations
with Republicans over the infrastructure bill

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:09.920
and this now puts the possibility of budget
reconciliation in focus.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.580
Joe Biden got off to a relatively fast start
immediately signing a bunch of executive orders,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.530
undoing some of the nonsense that Donald Trump
did, getting the one point nine trillion dollar

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:23.400
covid stimulus passed not as quickly as we
would have liked, but still by Washington

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:30.949
terms relatively quickly and then rolling
out a very successful vaccination program.

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:31.949
But what is next?

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:36.170
And we've been talking about, well, if it's
not going to be infrastructure, it's got to

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.180
be something else that's big.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:40.520
But infrastructure really seems like the way
to go.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:46.530
Joe Biden has been negotiating with Senator
Shelley Moore Capito and some other Republicans

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:47.840
over infrastructure legislation.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.440
And CBS News now reports that it's over.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:58.719
Joe Biden has said to Senator Cappato that
the last Republican proposal doesn't meet

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:02.239
the essential needs of our country to fix
roads and bridges.

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:07.729
It doesn't prepare the country for a future
that will be reliant on clean energy and it

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:08.729
doesn't create jobs.

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:11.869
And this is according to the White House press
secretary.

00:01:11.869 --> 00:01:14.670
And so what is now next?

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.840
And the answer is, I don't know.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.530
When you look at the details that have been
released of this negotiation that Joe Biden

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:29.270
had with with House and Senate lawmakers,
the he reduced his plan by over a trillion

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.270
dollars.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.890
That's a big concession from Joe Biden already.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:38.130
And then Republicans came up in new investments
by only one hundred and fifty billion.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:43.409
And this is the prototypical situation where
you negotiate and you negotiate and negotiate

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:45.000
with Republicans.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.040
You settle on something in the middle and
then they don't vote for it anyway.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.420
So what was all of the negotiation for now?

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.430
I am not against Joe Biden doing some discussion.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:02:00.159
I think it's fine that he did that because
now he can say we gave major concessions,

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:05.020
we sliced a trillion dollars off of this thing,
and then they wanted to give us essentially

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.020
nothing.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.020
And so we're moving on without them.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.119
I don't think that that's a waste of time.

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:16.029
What will become a waste of time is now if
they don't simply make this happen via budget

00:02:16.029 --> 00:02:19.010
reconciliation, can they actually do that?

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.010
I don't know.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:24.379
I don't know that Joe Manchin actually wants
the bill the way that it is.

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:31.239
And by the way, I mean, from an infrastructure
perspective, West Virginia is a disaster.

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:36.819
And by infrastructure, I mean traditional
infrastructure like bridges and tunnels and

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:39.200
roads in this type of thing.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.459
But broadband infrastructure, health care,
infrastructure, educational infrastructure

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:45.000
and on and on.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.199
If there is anybody in the Senate who should
be desperate for the biggest possible infrastructure

00:02:49.199 --> 00:02:51.090
package, it actually should be Joe Manchin.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.409
But as we talked about yesterday, I wasn't
kidding when I said Joe Manchin was going

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:55.590
to be a problem.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:59.719
And it's not even clear to me that Democrats
have the 50 votes in order to pass this via

00:02:59.719 --> 00:03:01.219
budget reconciliation.

00:03:01.219 --> 00:03:03.290
If we step back for a second.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:07.379
I've gotten emails from people saying, is
this something that can be done via budget

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:08.689
reconciliation?

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:13.890
Sometimes you'll hear some on the left say
Joe Biden should just do Medicare for all

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.310
through budget reconciliation.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.590
First of all, Joe Biden doesn't support Medicare
for all.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:23.109
But putting that aside, my best reading of
when budget reconciliation can be used would

00:03:23.109 --> 00:03:24.799
not allow it to be used for Medicare.

00:03:24.799 --> 00:03:31.359
For all that being said, there is no question
that budget reconciliation can be used to

00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:37.659
pass Joe Biden's infrastructure bill with
50 votes in the Senate rather than the 60

00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:39.409
you would normally need.

00:03:39.409 --> 00:03:43.709
But that's only if you have all 50 votes from
Democratic senators.

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:47.879
And it's not at this point clear that Joe
Biden actually does.

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:53.819
The New York Times also reporting on the stalled
negotiations not really stalled, ended negotiations,

00:03:53.819 --> 00:04:00.279
saying after weeks of failed efforts to bridge
deep divides, the president pulled the plug,

00:04:00.279 --> 00:04:05.849
turning to a bipartisan group to try to salvage
a chance at compromise.

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:09.069
My question is, how long do you keep going
at it from this approach?

00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:14.359
Because you end up with what are sometimes
these Pyrrhic victories where you do so much

00:04:14.359 --> 00:04:20.650
damage in the process of negotiating something
that Republicans will accept that you are

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.960
really compromising on your ability to pass
something that substantive.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.580
And with the current Republicans, as we saw
with the covid bill, sometimes you spend all

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.540
this time negotiating and they all vote against.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:37.840
It anyway, so my sense is that we're sort
of getting to the end here.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:44.080
The New York Times writes sort of pseudo optimistically,
the breakdown did not close off the possibility

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.379
of a bipartisan compromise entirely.

00:04:47.379 --> 00:04:50.729
And the White House signaled that the president
would continue seeking one.

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:54.840
He shifted his focus to a bipartisan group
of senators, senators who have been working

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.030
separately on an alternative, calling three
of them personally to cheer on efforts and

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.639
encourage them to work with top White House
officials to hammer out a deal.

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:07.210
But even if the group can agree on a plan
palatable to Joe Biden, there's no guarantee

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.720
enough Republicans would accept it.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.070
That that is exactly the point that needs
to be considered.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:19.159
How much longer are we doing this with Republicans
who, you know, it's conceivable that you get,

00:05:19.159 --> 00:05:24.310
I don't know, Joe Manchin and Mitt Romney
in a room and they come up with something

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.160
that they say they would support.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:32.129
But then do you still have 60 votes or are
you put in the position of having to go through

00:05:32.129 --> 00:05:35.780
budget reconciliation and then you're just
arguing with your own side?

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:36.960
You're arguing with Joe Manchin.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.409
Joe, listen, we have forty nine.

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:40.409
We need 50.

00:05:40.409 --> 00:05:41.860
What are you willing to do for us?

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:50.000
And this is the exact I mean, when you talk
about the type of partizan gridlock that sometimes

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.110
it's bipartisan gridlock, when you talk about
the type of partizan gridlock where the American

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:01.639
people just look at it and say, this is why
I am I am disillusioned about even voting.

00:06:01.639 --> 00:06:07.699
This is why we have voter turnout of 50, 55
percent at midterms, because on the one hand,

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:11.409
we understand that every two years we get
to say we don't like the people representing

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:13.389
us in the House of Representatives every four
years.

00:06:13.389 --> 00:06:14.879
You get to say that about the White House.

00:06:14.879 --> 00:06:19.389
Every six years you get to say that about
your senator from your particular state.

00:06:19.389 --> 00:06:24.189
But then when you see this stuff where Democrats
aren't even all on the same page, the things

00:06:24.189 --> 00:06:28.939
that Republicans and Democrats agree upon,
they don't have support for from the broader

00:06:28.939 --> 00:06:34.620
party, or you end up in a situation where
even those with the majority, in this case

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:40.870
Democrats, a slim majority, 50 50 with the
tie breaking vote, are negotiating against

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:41.870
themselves.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:42.870
And will Joe Manchin vote for it?

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.539
Will Sinema vote for it?

00:06:44.539 --> 00:06:46.530
I get being disillusioned with the system.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:51.099
And this is sort of like the worst of the
worst of the worst of American politics.

00:06:51.099 --> 00:06:53.039
I don't know whether this thing's going to
pass.

00:06:53.039 --> 00:07:01.469
If you said, you know, take a bet, I think
55, 45 something passes, how much it will

00:07:01.469 --> 00:07:03.830
look like Joe Biden's initial proposal.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:05.530
I have absolutely no idea.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.449
But I'm interested in hearing from you.

00:07:07.449 --> 00:08:06.289
Do you think this will go anywhere?

